<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jigonhsasee</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Jikonhsaseh, Jikonsase, Tsiokonsaseh, Mother of Nations</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jigonhsasee was an Iroquoian woman considered, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-peacemaker">Great Peacemaker</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiawatha">Hiawatha</a>, a co-founder of the Haudenosaunee Confederacy, dated by some between 1142 and 1450 and by others to about 1570–1600. According to Haudenosaunee oral tradition, she lived beside the warriors' path and gave warriors of every faction food and counsel at her hearth. She joined the Peacemaker's vision of a league in the form of a longhouse, and at the peace gathering he gave her the task of assigning men to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-council-qing">Grand Council</a> and gave women the power to choose the chiefs. Called the Mother of Nations, she became the Head Clan Mother, a name passed to her successors until the 1800s.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>The oral tradition is told in several versions. In some she already bore the name Jigonhsasee; in others the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-peacemaker">Great Peacemaker</a> gave it to her as a new name once he recognised her as an ally in making peace, saying that it meant New Face: &quot;It is in your countenance that a New Mind is manifest.&quot; Some versions describe her as a descendant of Sky Woman. While warriors of the warring factions ate at her hearth, she counselled them and learned their hearts.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-peacemaker">the Peacemaker</a> told her of his vision, she asked what form the confederacy would take. He answered that it would take the form of the longhouse, with many hearths, one for each family, all living as one household under one chief mother: &quot;Thinking will replace killing, and there shall be one commonwealth.&quot; She recognised the power in peace and joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiawatha">Hiawatha</a> and the Peacemaker to unite the Haudenosaunee.</p><p>Together, in the tradition, they freed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/onondaga-people">Onondaga</a> leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tadodaho">Tadodaho</a> of the evil that tormented him and named him Chief of Chiefs of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-council-qing">Grand Council</a> of the Haudenosaunee. The peace gathering at which she received her task was held under the Tree of Peace, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-peacemaker">the Peacemaker</a> called her Mother of Nations because she was the first ally of his peace movement.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>From the founding of the Confederacy until the 1800s her name passed to the leading Clan Mother of the Haudenosaunee, mainly through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seneca-people">Seneca</a> or Wendat women of the Wolf Clan. The last to hold it was Caroline Parker, sister of the Seneca Hoyaneh Ely S. Parker; because of the dispersal of the Haudenosaunee, no successor has been named since.</p><p>Some scholars have suggested that the Confederacy's constitution influenced those who drafted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-the-united-states">United States Constitution</a>; there is no academic consensus on this, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-senate">United States Senate</a> passed a resolution in 1988 acknowledging the influence. Late 20th-century genetic researchers named one of the five mitochondrial DNA haplogroups found among Native American populations Djigonasee in her honour.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jigonhsasee” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Tradition names <b>Jigonhsasee</b>, the Mother of Nations, among the founders of the League, but early researchers consulted only male informants, so the women’s oral tradition was largely lost to them.</blockquote><a href="/societies/haudenosaunee">Social Hierarchy of the Haudenosaunee</a></li></ul></section>
